{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SRC_005 and SRC_006 frame the analogy source as a mechanical or implicit model context; here the mechanism is statistical aggregation theory.","The source artifacts treat the checklist as a fixed artifact; this candidate treats checklist compliance as a stochastic process with decomposable bias and variance.","The target problem asks for a diagnostic within a single evaluation, which requires a within-evaluation statistical decomposition not present in the source analogies."],"overturn_conditions":["If the expert's intuition is shown to be decomposable into a shared prior plus idiosyncratic noise, the distinction between expert and panel collapses and the checklist is irrelevant.","If panel errors are shown to be perfectly correlated, the aggregation mechanism fails and the panel is equivalent to a single non-expert.","If the checklist is shown to be a source of idiosyncratic noise rather than a shared prior, the bias-variance decomposition proposed here is backwards."],"shared_structure":["SRC_005 discusses a panel of non-experts using a checklist, which is the target situation; the mechanism of aggregation noise reduction is implicit in the ensemble.","SRC_006 notes the panel uses an explicit checklist while source contexts involve implicit models, which mirrors the distinction between the shared prior (checklist) and individual interpretation noise.","Both this candidate and SRC_005/SRC_006 frame the panel's advantage as a property of the group process rather than individual competence."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006"],"transfer_claims":["The idea that a panel of non-experts can approximate expert performance transfers from SRC_005/SRC_006, with the refinement that the approximation quality depends on error correlation.","The distinction between explicit checklist and implicit models from SRC_006 transfers as the distinction between shared bias (from the checklist) and idiosyncratic noise (from